.TH CYAML_SET 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cyaml_set \- write a C scalar to the DOM leaf addressed by a path
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

ccol_retval_t cyaml_set(cyaml root, const char *path, val);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Detect the compile-time type of val with _Generic.  Write val as a
scalar value to the leaf node that path addresses.

The accepted C types for val, and the CYAML node type of each:
  bool                      -> CYAML_BOOL
  any integer type          -> CYAML_INTEGER (stored as long long),
                               or CYAML_FLOAT above LLONG_MAX
  float / double            -> CYAML_FLOAT (stored as double)
  char * / const char *     -> CYAML_STRING (heap copy made)
  char array (char buf[N])  -> CYAML_STRING
  string literal ("...")    -> CYAML_STRING
  void * (NULL included)    -> CYAML_NULL
  C23 nullptr               -> CYAML_NULL

Before C23 the macro true is the int 1, so cyaml_set(doc, k, true)
stores the CYAML_INTEGER 1.  Write (bool)true for a CYAML_BOOL.  In C23
true has the type bool and needs no cast. The library headers do not
include <stdbool.h> under C23; a C library whose <stdbool.h> still
defines true as 1 there (FreeBSD 14) brings the int back into every
file that includes it.

The macro rejects every other C type.  Such a type is long double, a
struct, or a pointer type other than char *, const char * and void *.
cyaml_set then returns ccol_invalid_args and leaves the target leaf
untouched.  For an unsupported type it creates no leaf that stays
behind as CYAML_NULL.  It also does not overwrite the value of a leaf
that already exists.

The macro copies val into a local whose type is the type of val after
array-to-pointer conversion, with top-level qualifiers removed.  A
char array, such as a buffer that snprintf(3) filled, therefore
arrives as a pointer to its first character, and a pointer that is
itself const, such as a static const char *const, arrives as an
ordinary pointer.  Each one gives the text of the string.

Signed integer types are sign-extended to long long.  Unsigned
integers in the range [0, LLONG_MAX] round-trip faithfully.  An
unsigned value above LLONG_MAX fits no long long, so the leaf becomes
CYAML_FLOAT and holds the nearest double.  That is the same node that
cyaml_parse(3) gives for the decimal literal of the value, which is
too large for the integer grammar of the core schema and resolves as a
float.  cyaml_double_val(3) then reads it; cyaml_int_val(3) does not.

A string value, and a leaf component that addresses a dictionary key,
must be well-formed UTF-8.  The macro returns ccol_invalid_args for
one that is not, and changes nothing.  Every Unicode character is
accepted; the serializer escapes one that a YAML document may not hold
raw.

For a dictionary leaf, the macro creates the key when it is absent.
The parent dictionary of that key must already exist.  For a list leaf
("#N"), the index must already be in range.  A list has no way to grow
by itself to fit an index that the caller chooses.  In both cases the
macro always replaces the type of a leaf that already exists.  It
deep-frees an existing list subtree or dictionary subtree before it
writes the new value.

The macro accepts and stores a double that is not finite (Inf, -Inf and
NaN).  cjson_set differs here, because it rejects these values.  They
serialize as .inf, -.inf and .nan.

A custom tag on an existing leaf survives the call.  A core-schema
tag survives only while it still names the type of the new value.
The macro drops a core-schema tag that the new value no longer
matches.  For example, on a node parsed from "port: !!int 8080",
cyaml_set(doc, "port", "auto") drops the !!int tag, and
cyaml_set(doc, "port", 9090) keeps it.  See cyaml_node_set_tag(3).

You must write (bool)true or (bool)false to get a CYAML_BOOL node.
Without that cast, the integer literal 1 or 0 gives a CYAML_INTEGER
node instead.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
root   Root cyaml handle.
path   Dot-separated path string.
       Escape sequences: '\e.' gives a literal '.', and '\e\e' gives a
       literal '\e'.  A '\e' before any other character stays
       unchanged.
val    C value.  The compiler resolves its type at compile time.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_retval_t: ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args; an empty path, a type that does not fit at a path
                      step, a bad "#N" list-index syntax, an empty
                      path component (nothing between two dots, a
                      leading dot, or a trailing dot), a C type for
                      val that is not one of the accepted types above,
                      a string value that is not well-formed UTF-8, or
                      a dictionary leaf component that is not
                      well-formed UTF-8.
ccol_key_not_found; a component of the path above the leaf does not
                      exist, or a leaf "#N" list index has correct
                      syntax but is out of range.
ccol_not_enough_memory; an allocation failure.
ccol_container_full; a new leaf dictionary key would take the parent
                      past the largest element count that it can
                      represent.  A real program does not reach that
                      count.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cyaml_set(doc, "server.port",  8080);
cyaml_set(doc, "server.name",  "prod");
cyaml_set(doc, "server.tls",   (bool)true);
cyaml_set(doc, "server.ratio", 0.95);

/* Replace a dictionary subtree with a scalar */
cyaml_set(doc, "server.options", 0);   /* deep-frees old subtree */

/* Set to null */
cyaml_set(doc, "server.deprecated", NULL);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_get (3),
.BR cyaml_node_set_tag (3)
